  "configuration_id": "config_001",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is no baseline performance for any modality or design choice. The entire configuration space spans RNA-only, image-only, and multimodal settings with multiple encoders, profile strategies, and aggregation/fusion options, making it critical to first establish a reference point. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest, most standard baseline—using a well-understood linear encoder (PCA) on a single RNA profile with no image input. It establishes how much predictive signal RNA alone provides under a minimal pipeline. 3) Why it is the best first use of budget: Starting from the simplest configuration maximizes the information gained per experiment. Every subsequent configuration (adding image, switching to multiprofile, using scvi/scgpt, changing aggregation/fusion) can be interpreted as a controlled modification relative to this baseline. This anchors the sequential search, allowing later experiments to attribute performance gains or losses to specific design decisions rather than to an unknown compound effect. It is also the fastest to run (no image encoder, no aggregation), conserving compute for later, more complex experiments."
}

